Identity and authority are verified by the constitutional record: AMLO held the presidency throughout 2022, with formal responsibility for federal appointments, executive implementation, security-force deployment, and diplomacy, subject to Congress, courts, federalism, treaty obligations, and operational chains. [Constitution of Mexico](https://www.oas.org/ext/Portals/33/Files/Member-States/en_mex-int-text-const.pdf)

The evidence environment is comparatively visible but uneven. Official presidential and Ayotzinapa records are extensive; independent coverage is strongest on militarization, journalists, oversight, and the Sedena leak. Press killings and self-censorship create visibility bias. Sedena-related material is institutionally concentrated, and allegations must not be treated as findings. National indicators remain context, not ruler attribution.

Chapter gaps:

- 1B–3B: case-level federal security, disappearance, torture, migration, and remedy records.
- 4B: electoral reform, INE/SCJN litigation, oversight appointments, transparency, surveillance, and press rhetoric.
- 5B–6B: realized growth, inflation, wages, poverty, labor, health, education, vaccination, and social-program implementation.
- 7B: personal-integrity nexus only—benefits, disclosures, appointments, responses, correction, concealment, or protected networks.
- 8B: implementation, procurement, audits, administrative capacity, infrastructure, security, health, and energy outcomes.

Sources opened and retained: OAS constitutional text; Mexico Presidency Ayotzinapa release; HRW; CPJ; Freedom House; El País; USTR; OECD. Government security transcripts were consulted as contrary/context material, not independent corroboration.
